Prologue: The Republic Yet to Come

In an age when humanity stands between the achievements of its ancestors and the unimaginable possibilities of its descendants, a new vision shall arise.

The old questions shall no longer suffice.

Nations shall ask not merely how they might survive, but how they might flourish. Universities shall ask not merely what humanity has discovered, but what remains undiscovered. Governments shall ask not merely how wealth might be distributed, but how the production of abundance might be accelerated until poverty itself becomes an institution of the past.

And from this ambition shall emerge the idea of FuturUSA.

Not a republic founded upon the supremacy of one people over another, nor a government ordained by ancestry, wealth, or inherited privilege, but a proposed civilization of scientific ambition, democratic legitimacy, technological abundance, and human flourishing.

Its founding vision shall rest upon one principle:

The future must not merely happen to humanity. Humanity must learn to build its future deliberately.

And in the symbolic tradition of its founding mythology, five names shall become associated with the stewardship of this great experiment:

Sophia KIANNI. Phoebe Gates. Parmita Mishra. Ali Debow. Chiamaka Igwe.

Five individuals. Five distinct identities. One imagined constitutional project.

The prophecy shall call them the Five Co-Presidents.

Not because destiny has already appointed them, but because the future imagined by FuturUSA requires a model of leadership in which no single person possesses all the wisdom necessary to govern a civilization.

Their story begins not with a throne, but with a question:

What would America become if its highest political ambition were to maximize the flourishing of every human being?

