Chapter VIII: The Contribution Game

The economic philosophy of FuturUSA shall be known as the Contribution Game.

Its foundational question shall be:

How can a society reward the creation of genuine value while ensuring that the capacity to contribute is not restricted to a privileged minority?

The Contribution Game shall treat civilization as a cooperative enterprise involving scientists, entrepreneurs, teachers, engineers, caregivers, artists, public servants, and citizens performing countless other forms of productive and socially valuable work.

Its central insight shall be that the prosperity of a society depends upon both individual initiative and the institutions that allow people to cooperate.

FuturUSA shall therefore experiment with systems that reward innovation, encourage voluntary collaboration, finance public goods, and expand access to education and productive tools.

It shall investigate how artificial intelligence might reduce administrative burdens, how startups might solve previously expensive problems, and how improved public institutions might turn technological progress into better living standards.

Its economic success shall be evaluated through measurable outcomes.

Does the system increase productivity?

Does it improve health and education?

Does it encourage the creation of useful businesses?

Does it reduce avoidable poverty?

Does it protect individual freedom?

Does it improve the prospects of future generations?

These questions shall be investigated rather than answered by ideological declaration.

The Contribution Game shall not assume that every contribution can be measured perfectly in money, nor that every socially valuable activity belongs in a market.

Caregiving, scientific exploration, civic participation, and the maintenance of trusted institutions may produce benefits that prices alone cannot adequately represent.

The purpose of the game shall be to improve the rules of cooperation so that individual ambition and collective flourishing become increasingly compatible.

Its maxim shall be:

Reward the creation of value, expand the opportunity to contribute, and build institutions through which cooperation produces greater prosperity than isolation.

